[b]Off Duty[/b] gives you a reason to let your regulars take a break. Operators get tired from back-to-back missions and recover while they're off duty. You can still send your best people out tired, but they'll feel it in combat.

[heading]How It Works[/heading]
[list]
[*]Fatigue starts counting [b]after cycle 5[/b] by default, once your roster is big enough to rotate. Until then, missions add no fatigue.[/*]
[*]Each operator who deploys gains [b]2 fatigue per mission[/b] by default.[/*]
[*]Operators recover [b]2 fatigue per strategy turn off duty[/b] by default (3 on Easy, 1.5 on Hard). If they're away on an operation, their fatigue stays where it is until they return.[/*]
[*]On the default settings, [b]two squads taking turns stay rested[/b]. Each mission in a row makes an operator one tier more tired.[/*]
[*]Fatigue determines their penalties for the next mission. [b]It never stops you from deploying them.[/b][/*]
[*]If a mission requires an operator, their fatigue counts as [b]half[/b] when deciding their penalties, rounded down. Their actual fatigue stays the same, and the mission still adds fatigue as usual.[/*]
[*]Every squad slot shows a banner: RESTED, REQUIRED, TIRED, EXHAUSTED or SPENT. Hover over it to see the operator's fatigue, penalties and recovery time. Roster portraits also mark tired and required operators.[/*]
[*]During a mission, fatigue penalties appear under the health bar and in the Inspect panel. The hit chance penalty is listed as "Penalty from Operation" in the hit breakdown.[/*]
[*]Fatigue is separate from injuries. The game's injury system works as usual.[/*]
[/list]

[heading]Tiers[/heading]
These are the fatigue ranges and penalties (the same on every preset):
[list]
[*][b]Tired[/b] (2–3 fatigue): −5% hit chance, −5% max health[/*]
[*][b]Exhausted[/b] (4–5): −10% hit chance, −10% max health, 10% chance each turn to lose 1 AP[/*]
[*][b]Spent[/b] (6–7): −20% hit chance, −20% max health, −10% movement, 15% chance each turn to lose 1 AP[/*]
[/list]
The penalties were tuned over hundreds of AI-vs-AI test missions.

[heading]Settings[/heading]
Install [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/starwarszerocompany/mods/149]Mixamoo Mod Config Manager[/url] to adjust these settings in game:
[list]
[*][b]Preset[/b]: how fast operators recover off duty: Easy (3 per turn), Standard (2, the default) or Hard (1.5)[/*]
[*][b]Fatigue per mission[/b]: 0–4[/*]
[*][b]Fatigue starts after cycle[/b]: 1–20[/*]
[*][b]AP loss[/b]: turn the chance to lose 1 AP on or off[/*]
[/list]
Without the config manager, Off Duty uses the Standard preset, 2 fatigue per mission, fatigue starting after cycle 5 and AP loss enabled.

[heading]Requirements[/heading]
[list]
[*][b]Star Wars: Zero Company[/b][/*]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/starwarszerocompany/mods/9]UE4SS for Star Wars Zero Company[/url][/*]
[*]Optional: [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/starwarszerocompany/mods/149]Mixamoo Mod Config Manager[/url], for the settings page[/*]
[/list]

[heading]Installation[/heading]
The download contains [b]OffDuty[/b] and [b]OffDuty_Assets[/b]. [b]Install both[/b]; the mod needs its asset files to work.

[b]Mod manager[/b]
Install the ZIP with either of these mod managers:
[list]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/starwarszerocompany/mods/29]Zero Mod Manager[/url]: choose [b]Install all components[/b].[/*]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/starwarszerocompany/mods/121]Zero Company Mod Command[/url]: it adds Off Duty and Off Duty — Assets as two entries.[/*]
[/list]
Make sure both are enabled.

[b]Manual installation[/b]
Install UE4SS first, then close the game.
[list=1]
[*]Extract the [b]OffDuty[/b] folder into:
[code]SWZeroCompany/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/Mods/[/code][/*]
[*]Copy [b]OffDuty_P.pak[/b], [b]OffDuty_P.ucas[/b] and [b]OffDuty_P.utoc[/b] from [b]OffDuty_Assets[/b] into the folder below. Create [b]~mods[/b] if it's missing.
[code]SWZeroCompany/Content/Paks/~mods/[/code][/*]
[/list]
The package includes [b]enabled.txt[/b]. If your UE4SS setup doesn't pick it up, add this line to [b]ue4ss/Mods/mods.txt[/b]:
[code]OffDuty : 1[/code]
[b]Updating:[/b] close the game first, then install the new ZIP the same way.

[b]Uninstalling[/b]
Disable or remove both parts in your mod manager. For a manual uninstall, delete the [b]OffDuty[/b] folder and the three [b]OffDuty_P[/b] files. Your saves will still load, and operators will stop gaining fatigue.

[heading]Known Issues[/heading]
[list]
[*]Restart the game after installing or updating Off Duty. Don't use UE4SS's "Reload All Mods"; it can crash or freeze the game.[/*]
[*]Guest units who aren't on your roster (such as Rex) don't gain fatigue.[/*]
[/list]

[heading]Troubleshooting[/heading]
If something goes wrong, please include:
[list]
[*]Your game build[/*]
[*]Your UE4SS version[/*]
[*]Your other installed mods[/*]
[*]What you were doing and what happened[/*]
[*][code]UE4SS.log[/code] (the startup and crash log) and [code]off_duty.log[/code] (in the installed [b]OffDuty[/b] folder)[/*]
[/list]
The logs record technical details, including folder paths on your PC. You can look them over before sharing; they don't include screenshots, saves or personal files.

[heading]Source Code and Issue Reports[/heading]
You can find the source and technical docs on [url=https://github.com/chatterchats/ZComOffDuty]GitHub[/url]. Report problems there or in the Bugs tab here on Nexus.

